{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Los Angeles Dodgers arrived with championship ambitions and delivered a 100-win season powered by an elite offense and a rotation anchored by Yoshinobu Yamamoto. Opening Day set the tone immediately, as Will Smith's two-run homer in late March announced that this was a team built to win close games. Through April, the Dodgers played .615 ball, with Max Muncy providing pop and a wild April 27th comeback — fueled by Shohei Ohtani and Kyle Tucker — encapsulating the lineup's relentless depth. They were never truly out of a game.\n\nMay and June were the Dodgers' finest stretch, going 36-19 over those two months and pulling away in the NL West. The offense exploded with Dalton Rushing's clutch line-drive single in a pivotal June 19th win and Freddie Freeman's continued presence in the middle of the order. Even Tanner Scott's two rough outings — surrendering home runs against Philadelphia and Arizona — couldn't derail a team that was pitching, hitting, and defending at an elite level. The bullpen's occasional cracks were plastered over by an offense that ranked among the best in the National League.\n\nAugust brought the season's darkest chapter. Edwin Díaz surrendered a go-ahead home run on August 7th and was torched again the following night, and Tanner Scott continued to be a liability in high-leverage spots. The Dodgers went 13-14 in August — their only losing month — and suddenly questions about the backend of the bullpen loomed large heading into the stretch run. But September answered every doubt emphatically. The team went 18-7, with Teoscar Hernández, Mookie Betts, and Tommy Edman delivering massive hits to clinch the division and carry the Dodgers into October with momentum.\n\nThe postseason began promisingly, as Los Angeles dispatched their Division Series opponent with contributions from unlikely heroes like Enrique Hernández and Andy Pages. But the NLCS proved to be a bridge too far. Milwaukee pushed the Dodgers to their limits, and despite Will Smith's clutch doubles and Shohei Ohtani's three-run home run in Game 4, the pitching staff could not hold leads when it mattered most. Christian Yelich and the Brewers battered Yamamoto and the bullpen in critical moments, and a 100-win season ended one series short of the World Series — a stunning, deflating conclusion to what had been one of the most complete regular seasons in recent Dodgers history.",
